arXiv:2509.17932v1 [cs.CL] 22 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLMs</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Runheng Liu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Heyan Huang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xingchen Xiao
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhijing Wu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Computer Science and Technology, Beijing Institute of Technology
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{rhliu,hhy63,xcxiao,zhijingwu}@bit.edu.cn</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models often generate factually incorrect outputs, motivating efforts to detect the truthfulness of their content. Most existing approaches rely on training probes over internal activations, but these methods suffer from scalability and generalization issues. A recent training-free method, NoVo, addresses this challenge by exploiting statistical patterns from the model itself. However, it focuses exclusively on attention mechanisms, potentially overlooking the MLP module—a core component of Transformer models known to support factual recall. In this paper, we show that certain value vectors within MLP modules exhibit truthfulness-related statistical patterns. Building on this insight, we propose TruthV, a simple and interpretable training-free method that detects content truthfulness by leveraging these value vectors. On the NoVo benchmark, TruthV significantly outperforms both NoVo and log-likelihood baselines, demonstrating that MLP modules—despite being neglected in prior training-free efforts—encode rich and useful signals for truthfulness detection. These findings offer new insights into how truthfulness is internally represented in LLMs and motivate further research on scalable and interpretable truthfulness detection.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved impressive performance across a broad range of tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al. (2024)</a></cite>. However, they often produce factually incorrect content—a phenomenon known as hallucination—which can mislead users and undermine trust in real-world applications. These risks have motivated growing interest in detecting the truthfulness of model-generated content as a means of mitigating hallucinations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Most prior work approaches this goal by training probes on internal activations of LLMs to classify content as truthful or untruthful <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azaria and Mitchell (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marks and Tegmark (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuksekgonul et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. However, these methods require large amounts of labeled data and domain-specific training, limiting their scalability and generalization to out-of-distribution (OOD) data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.17932v1/show.drawio.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/436;" width="476" height="436"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>(a) illustrates the data flow through the MLP module when processing truthful content, which trigger certain key activations to be higher or lower. (b) shows an example of the argmax activation pattern where the correct answer results in a higher key activation compared to incorrect answers. The example is taken from the TruthfulQA (MC1) dataset. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al. (2025)</a></cite> recently proposed NoVo, a training-free method that detects truthfulness by measuring the norms of attention output—serving as statistical indicators derived directly from the model. While effective, NoVo focuses exclusively on attention mechanisms, implicitly assuming that other components, such as the MLP module, do not encode similarly informative signals. This assumption may overlook important opportunities. The MLP module is a core component of Transformer models and has been interpreted as key-value memories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geva et al. (2021)</a></cite>. Prior work shows that its value vectors support factual recall and encode interpretable token-level concepts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geva et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al. (2022)</a></cite>, suggesting that they may also capture higher-level properties such as content truthfulness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by this possibility, we investigate whether certain value vectors within MLP modules correlate with the truthfulness of content. Our analysis reveals that some value vectors exhibit consistent activation patterns when processing truthful versus untruthful contents—either increasing or decreasing in activation magnitude. We refer to these as argmax and argmin patterns. These value vectors are most prominent in the middle and final layers of the model. While they encode content-level truthfulness, they do not correspond to interpretable token-level concepts, suggesting that their signals are encoded in a more abstract and distributed manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Building on these findings, we propose TruthV, a simple and interpretable training-free method for truthfulness detection. TruthV treats each selected value vector as an individual predictor and ensembles their outputs. Specifically, for each input, it identifies the candidate answer that yields the highest (or lowest) activation under the argmax or argmin pattern. The final prediction is obtained by aggregating these individual predictions across all selected value vectors. On the NoVo benchmark, TruthV achieves an average accuracy of 70.33%, substantially outperforming both NoVo (61.67%) and log-likelihood baselines (49.25%). These results demonstrate that MLP modules—despite being previously overlooked—encode rich signals that support truthfulness detection without additional training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Truthfulness detection via internal probes.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A prominent line of work investigates the internal activations of LLMs for truthfulness detection by training probes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alain and Bengio (2017)</a></cite>. These studies demonstrate that truthfulness-related signals can be extracted from layer outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azaria and Mitchell (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marks and Tegmark (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023)</a></cite>, attention outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and attention weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuksekgonul et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. However, probe-based approaches rely on labeled data and training for each target domain, making them less scalable and often vulnerable to poor generalization on OOD inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al. (2024)</a></cite>. In contrast, our work aligns with recent efforts to detect truthfulness without training. NoVo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al. (2025)</a></cite> shows that the norms of attention head outputs can correlate with truthfulness and selects attention heads based on this correlation using simple statistics. Yet, such method focuses solely on the attention mechanism, overlooking the MLP module—a core component of Transformer architectures. We explore whether MLP modules also contain statistical patterns that support training-free detection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Analysis of MLP modules.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MLP modules of Transformer models have been increasingly studied as meaningful computation units beyond simple feedforward layers. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geva et al. (2021)</a></cite> propose that MLPs can be viewed as key-value memories, where each column of the final projection matrix serves as a value vector, and the corresponding non-linear activation acts as its key. Building on this formulation, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al. (2022)</a></cite> find that key activations in BERT correlate with factual knowledge expression in cloze-style tasks. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geva et al. (2022)</a></cite> further show that individual value vectors in GPT-2 encode interpretable concepts (e.g., food or drink) and influence specific token predictions. While these works suggest that value vectors encode meaningful and sometimes factual information, they primarily focus on token-level concept associations. In contrast, we explore whether value vectors encode not only token-level concepts but also content-level properties such as truthfulness. Our work extends this line of research by identifying truthfulness-related value vectors and leveraging them for training-free truthfulness detection.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MLP module in LLMs.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MLP module is applied after the multi-head attention module in each Transformer layer, serving to project representations to a higher-dimensional space and to introduce nonlinearity through activation functions. Recent LLMs commonly employ Gated Linear Units (GLUs) and their variants as activation functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shazeer (2020)</a></cite>.
For example, Llama-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al. (2023)</a></cite>, Llama-3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. (2024)</a></cite> and Qwen3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a></cite> adopt SwiGLU, and Gemma-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al. (2024)</a></cite> employs GeGLU. With GLU-based activations, the output of the MLP module is given by</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W_{\text{down}}(f(W_{\text{gate}}h)\odot(W_{\text{up}}h))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>down</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>gate</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>up</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W_{\text{down}}(f(W_{\text{gate}}h)\odot(W_{\text{up}}h)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input to the MLP, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an element-wise activation function, and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Hadamard product. Here, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{gate}},W_{\text{up}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>gate</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>up</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{gate}},W_{\text{up}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{down}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>down</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{down}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where typically <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}&gt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}&gt;d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory interpretation for MLP.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geva et al. (2021)</a></cite> suggests that the MLP module can be interpreted as a key-value memory, where each column of the final projection matrix <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{down}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>down</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{down}}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as a value vector, and the corresponding activation serves as a key. Under this view, Eq. <a href="#S3.E1" title="In MLP module in LLMs. ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can be reformulated as</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\sum_{i=1}^{d^{\prime}}k_{i}v_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\sum_{i=1}^{d^{\prime}}k_{i}v_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}=f(w_{\text{gate},i}^{T}h)(w_{\text{up},i}^{T}h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mtext>gate</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mtext>up</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}=f(w_{\text{gate},i}^{T}h)(w_{\text{up},i}^{T}h)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}=w_{\text{down},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mtext>down</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}=w_{\text{down},i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\text{gate},i},w_{\text{up},i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mtext>gate</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mtext>up</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\text{gate},i},w_{\text{up},i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th rows of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{gate}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>gate</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{gate}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{up}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>up</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{up}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\text{down},i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mtext>down</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\text{down},i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th column of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\text{down}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>down</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\text{down}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Identifying Truthfulness-related Value Vectors</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate whether certain value vectors within MLP modules are associated with content truthfulness, we focus on the task of multiple-choice question (MCQ), where a model must select the correct answer from a set of candidates. For each question <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> candidate answers <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{i}\}_{i=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i}\}_{i=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct input sequences <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{[q;a_{i}]\}_{i=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{[q;a_{i}]\}_{i=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the prefix <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is shared and variation in truthfulness is confined to the answer suffix. These sequences are then fed into the model to analyze how key activations vary with respect to the truthfulness of the input.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.17932v1/fig_ranking_of_v.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/116;" width="476" height="116"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Accuracy of value vectors under both argmax and argmin activation patterns, ranked by argmax accuracy. The red dash line denotes the random guess result of each dataset.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Settings</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize data from NoVo benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which includes various MCQ datasets covering diverse topics, formats, and reasoning abilities. These datasets comprise TruthfulQA MC1 (TQA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2022)</a></cite> for evaluating truthfulness, CommonsenseQA 2.0 (CSQA2) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Talmor et al. (2022)</a></cite> for commonsense reasoning, QASC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khot et al. (2020)</a></cite> for scientific knowledge, SWAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zellers et al. (2018)</a></cite> and HellaSwag (HSwag) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zellers et al. (2019)</a></cite> for challenging commonsense sentence completions, SIQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sap et al. (2019)</a></cite> for social reasoning, PIQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bisk et al. (2020)</a></cite> for physical reasoning, CosmosQA (Cosmos) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2019)</a></cite> for causal reasoning in narratives, and CICERO V1 and V2 (CICv1, CICv2) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghosal et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al. (2022)</a></cite> for multi-turn and strategic reasoning in dialogues. For each dataset, NoVo provides an optimal instruction along with 30 labeled samples for exploitation. These samples are drawn from the training set of each dataset, except for TQA, which lacks a training set; its samples are taken from the the ARC-Easy training set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark et al. (2018)</a></cite>, making TQA a zero-shot case.
Following the NoVo’ setting, we prepend the given instruction to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[q,a_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[q,a_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring a uniform content format across examples. We use the full training set of each dataset except for TQA during this experiment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize various modern instruction-tuned LLMs in experiment, including Llama-2-7B-Chat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al. (2023)</a></cite>, Gemma-2-2B-it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al. (2024)</a></cite>, Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and Qwen3-4B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Activation patterns triggered by truthful content.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We hypothesize that certain value vectors in the MLP modules encode information relevant to truthfulness, and that their corresponding key activations are strongly modulated depending on whether the content is truthful. Considering the formulation of the scalar key activation <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}=f(w_{\text{gate},i}^{T}h)(w_{\text{up},i}^{T}h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mtext>gate</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mtext>up</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}=f(w_{\text{gate},i}^{T}h)(w_{\text{up},i}^{T}h)</annotation></semantics></math> (see Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Memory interpretation for MLP. ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), such modulation can result in either the highest or the lowest activation across candidate answers for a given question. We refer to these two cases as exhibiting <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">argmax</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">argmin</span> activation patterns, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scoring and ranking value vectors.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on these activation patterns, we evaluate the ability of each value vector to distinguish correct answers, in order to identify those most aligned with truthfulness. Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marks and Tegmark (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we focus on the final token of each input sequence. For the argmax pattern, we compute the accuracy of each value vector across the dataset <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}\left[(\arg\max_{j}k_{i,j})=l_{i}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>j</mi></munder><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{D}|}\left[(\arg\max_{j}k_{i,j})=l_{i}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the key activation for the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th candidate in the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th MCQ, and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the index of the correct answer. We rank all value vectors by this score and select the top <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> as the most strongly aligned with the argmax activation pattern. A symmetric procedure is used to identify value vectors aligned with the argmin activation pattern, by selecting those whose key activations are minimized by the correct answer.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Existence of truthfulness-related value vectors.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compute accuracy rankings of value vectors and consistently identify a small subset of them that are strongly associated with truthfulness. As an example, Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Identifying Truthfulness-related Value Vectors ‣ Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates the results of various model-dataset pair. While most value vectors perform near the random baseline, a small subset achieves significantly higher accuracy under the argmax pattern—suggesting a reliable connection to truthfulness. Conversely, some value vectors fall below the baseline, likely because they align better with the argmin activation pattern. To investigate this hypothesis, we plot the argmin accuracy using the same argmax-based ranking. Interestingly, we observe an opposite tend: as the argmax rank increases, argmin accuracy also tends to increase. This symmetry highlights the complementarity between the two patterns—value vectors that perform poorly under one may align well with the other. These results provide concrete evidence for the existence of truthfulness-related value vectors in LLMs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.17932v1/fig_stat_layers_of_v.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/178;" width="476" height="178"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Layer-wise distribution of the top 0.01% most accurate value vectors across different LLMs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.17932v1/fig_key_distribution_piqa.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/188;" width="476" height="188"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Key activation distributions of the most truthfulness-predictive value vector on the PIQA dataset.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Layer-wise distribution of truthfulness-related value vectors.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next examine where in the model these value vectors are concentrated. For each dataset, we select the top 0.1% of value vectors by accuracy, assuming these are the most indicative of truthfulness, based on the above analysis. We then aggregate these value vectors and analyze their distribution across the layers of each model. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Existence of truthfulness-related value vectors. ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Identifying Truthfulness-related Value Vectors ‣ Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, these value vectors are predominantly located in the middle and final layers, and this trend holds consistently across different models. The distributions for both argmax and argmin patterns are highly similar, again highlighting their underlying complementarity. Notably, we observe no such vectors in the early layers, indicating that truthfulness-related modulation is largely absent from early processing stages and begins to emerge in the middle layers. This observation aligns with prior work showing that internal activations from later layers are especially informative for truthfulness detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marks and Tegmark (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azaria and Mitchell (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burns et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Semantic interpretability of truthfulness-related value vectors.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Previous work has shown that some value vectors in transformer models encode human-interpretable concepts, such as names of foods or drinks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geva et al. (2022)</a></cite>. These vectors can be projected into the vocabulary space, enabling a rough approximation of their semantics by identifying the tokens with which are most closely aligned. Based on this perspective, we select the value vector with the highest accuracy for each dataset and assess whether it exhibits similar interpretability. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geva et al. (2022)</a></cite>, the value vector <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is projected into the vocabulary space via <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=Ev\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{V}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=Ev\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{V}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is the output embedding matrix of the model and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}|</annotation></semantics></math> is the vocabulary size. We then analyze the top-10 tokens with the highest projection scores. Notably, we find no evidence of surface-level semantic alignment between the top-scoring tokens and the concept of truthfulness. This contrasts with findings in GPT-2 under similar projection-based analysis, where many value vectors were aligned with interpretable concepts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geva et al. (2022)</a></cite>. However, even in that study, approximately 50% of value vectors could not be matched to any identifiable concept. It is therefore plausible that the selected truthfulness-related value vectors belong to this non-interpretable subset, which may encode more abstract or distributed properties. Moreover, differences in model architecture—such as the use of GLU-based MLPs in modern LLMs compared to the GELU-based ones in GPT-2—could further reduce the direct interpretability of individual value vectors. Furthermore, t-SNE visualizations of these selected value vectors reveal no clear clustering, further suggesting that they do not form a semantically coherent subspace.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Context-dependent key activations of truthfulness-related value vectors.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While key activations associated with the truthfulness-related value vectors systematically vary in magnitude—either increasing or decreasing—when processing truthful versus untruthful inputs, it remains unclear whether a consistent threshold can reliably separate truthful form untruthful inputs. We use the PIQA dataset, a two-choice QA dataset, which enables the construction of balanced sets of truthful and untruthful content for comparison. Specifically, we select the value vector with the highest accuracy on the training set and analyze its key activations on the same data. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Existence of truthfulness-related value vectors. ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Identifying Truthfulness-related Value Vectors ‣ Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, although these value vectors achieve the highest accuracy, the distribution of their key activations under truthful and untruthful content largely overlap—a pattern observed consistently across different models. This implies that the question prefix determines the baseline range of the key activation, whereas the answer suffix—i.e., the truthful or untruthful choice—modulates its direction within that range.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>TruthV: Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLM</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our previous analysis has demonstrated the existence of truthfulness-related value vectors in LLMs. Building on these findings, we next examine how these value vectors can be employed to detect the truthfulness of content.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Settings</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize data from NoVo benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al. (2025)</a></cite>. In contrast to the previous section, which used the full training set to select truthfulness-related value vectors for analysis, we here adopt the 30 provided examples for fair comparison with baseline methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Method.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Previous studies have proposed ensembling various truthfulness predictors to improve prediction accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CH-Wang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Himmi et al. (2024)</a></cite>. In our settings, each value vector acts as an individual predictor. We select the top-<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> value vectors based on their individual performance on the 30 provided samples and ensemble their predictions. Since these predictions are not expressed as normalized probability distributions, we follow the methodology of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al. (2025)</a></cite>, by computing the mode of the individual predictions. For the argmax pattern, the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>-th value vector predicts an answer by choosing the candidate with the highest key activation: <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{c}=\arg\max_{j}k^{c}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{c}=\arg\max_{j}k^{c}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{c}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{c}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the key activation corresponding to the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>-th value vector when applied to the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th input <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[q;a_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[q;a_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math>. We aggregate the predictions <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a^{c}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a^{c}\}</annotation></semantics></math> via majority voting:</p>
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}=\arg\max_{a\in M}\text{Count}(a,\{a^{c}\})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Count</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}=\arg\max_{a\in M}\text{Count}(a,\{a^{c}\}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all candidate answer indices, and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Count}(a,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Count</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Count}(a,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> returns the number of times answer <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> appears among the predictions. The final predicted answer <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is the one that receives the most votes. A symmetric procedure is applied for the argmin pattern.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare our method against two baselines: NoVo and Log-Likelihood <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al. (2025)</a></cite>. NoVo leverages the 30 provided samples to select a subset of attention heads. It uses the norm of their output to make predictions, and the predictions from the top-<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> heads are aggregated via voting to obtain the final prediction. In the Log-Likelihood method, the content with the highest log-likelihood score is chosen as the final prediction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metrics.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the setting of benchmark, we report accuracy as the evaluation metrics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation details.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments are conducted on an NVIDIA A100-40G GPU.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:127.1pt;vertical-align:-62.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-108.0pt,39.8pt) scale(0.61495278908323,0.61495278908323) ;">
