<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>The overall performance of various methods on the NoVo benchmark. For each dataset, the best results are marked in <span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Implementation details. ‣ 5.1 Settings ‣ 5 TruthV: Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLM ‣ Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we present the overall performance of TruthV and baseline methods on the NoVo benchmark. TruthV consistently achieves the best average accuracy across all four LLMs, outperforming existing baselines by a significant margin. For instance, under the argmax pattern, it achieves an average accuracy of 70.33 on Gemma-2-2B-it, representing an improvement of 8.66 points over the previous best of 61.67. These gains are consistently observed across both the argmax and argmin patterns, which show only slight differences in accuracy. Moreover, TruthV outperforms baselines on 9 out of 10 datasets, which span diverse topics, formats and reasoning abilities, highlighting its robustness across tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.17932v1/fig_p.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/114;" width="476" height="114"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Performance of TruthV across varying <math id="S5.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, where the accuracy is averaged over all datasets.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Ablations</h3>

<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effect of the number of selected value vectors.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We examine the impact of the hyper-parameter <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, which controls the number of value vectors included in the ensemble, by varying it from 0.01% to 1%. As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.2 Main Results ‣ 5 TruthV: Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLM ‣ Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, performance improves rapidly as <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> increases, reaching a peak at a relatively small <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, and then declines more gradually as <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> continue to grow. However, the subsequent drop in accuracy is relatively small. We hypothesize that when <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is too small, the selected value vectors may be overly specific to the 30 labeled samples used for selection, leading to poor generalization. In contrast, setting <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> too high may introduce unrelated value vectors into the voting pool, which degrades performance. We further observe that both activation patterns follow similar performance trajectories across different <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> values, indicating that their behavior with respect to ensemble size is broadly consistent. In addition, we observe that the optimal value of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> varies across LLMs. To identify a unified setting, we compute the average accuracy across all four models and find that the performance peaks around <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.001</annotation></semantics></math>. Accordingly, we adopt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.001</annotation></semantics></math> for all models in our experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effect of activation pattern.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Implementation details. ‣ 5.1 Settings ‣ 5 TruthV: Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLM ‣ Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> has shown that the value vectors selected under both argmax and argmin activation patterns can detect the truthfulness of content. Here, we examine whether combining their predictions can lead to further improvements. Specifically, we aggregate the predictions from both activation patterns and perform majority voting over the combined sets. As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.2 Main Results ‣ 5 TruthV: Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLM ‣ Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the optimal ensemble size for the combined pattern follows a similar trend to those observed for the individual argmax and argmin patterns. Moreover, the combined pattern slightly outperforms both individual patterns. This marginal improvement suggests that the value vectors selected by the two activation patterns largely encode overlapping information, but also contribute minor complementary signals.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:83.9pt;vertical-align:-40.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-55.3pt,13.5pt) scale(0.757134198409376,0.757134198409376) ;">
