<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Results of pair-wise generalization on Gemma-2-2B-it, under the argmax activation pattern.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Analysis</h3>

<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Efficiency of selection.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results of the above experiments demonstrate that even with only 30 samples, the selected value vectors yield competitive performance. Intuitively, incorporating more samples into the selection process should improve the performance of TruthV. Therefore, we further use the full training set of each dataset for selection (except for TQA, which lacks a training set). Since training set sizes vary across datasets, we search <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> from 0.01% to 1% and report the best result for each dataset. We adopt the argmax activation pattern throughout this experiment. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Effect of activation pattern. ‣ 5.3 Ablations ‣ 5 TruthV: Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLM ‣ Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, using the full training set consistently leads to better performance compared to using only 30 samples. However, in a few settings (e.g., Llama-2-7B-Chat on SWAG), the improvement is marginal or even slightly negative. This suggests that many truthfulness-related value vectors are already ranked near the top with just 30 samples, such that adding more data provides limited benefit. In the most scenarios, while using more data does improve the ranking of truthfulness-related value vectors, TruthV already achieves competitive results even under the low-resource setting, suggesting that these value vectors are salient and can be reliably identified with limited supervision.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generalization ability of truthfulness-related value vectors.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior studies have found that probing-based methods often exhibit limited generalization across tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">CH-Wang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a></cite>. We further investigate how well the selected value vectors generalize across datasets. Specifically, we adopt the argmax activation pattern and use the full training set from each source dataset to select value vectors. These vectors are then evaluated on the validation sets of other target datasets. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Effect of activation pattern. ‣ 5.3 Ablations ‣ 5 TruthV: Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLM ‣ Training-free Truthfulness Detection via Value Vectors in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, generalization performance consistently exceeds the random baseline, indicating that value vectors selected from one dataset can transfer to others to some extent. However, in most cases, this cross-dataset performance remains lower than that achieved by selecting value vectors using just 30 samples of the target dataset. These findings suggest that truthfulness-related value vectors encode a mixture of task-general and task-specific information.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we investigate whether the MLP modules of LLMs encode statistical patterns related to content truthfulness. Building on the key-value memory perspective of MLP modules, we identify specific value vectors whose key activations consistently vary between truthful and untruthful inputs. We propose TruthV, a simple and interpretable training-free method to detect content truthfulness. Experiments on the NoVo benchmark show that TruthV significantly outperforms both NoVo and log-likelihood baselines. Our findings demonstrate that MLP modules contain interpretable patterns that correlate with truthfulness and can be directly exploited without additional training. This work provides new insights into the internal structure of LLMs and highlights the potential of leveraging statistical patterns for scalable and interpretable truthfulness detection.</p>
</div>
</section>
